---
import fs from 'node:fs';
import path from 'node:path';
const SITE = 'https://realgitrank.bharatdn.com';
const CORRECTION_URL = 'https://github.com/ErBharatMalhotra/RealGitRank/issues/new?title=Correction%20or%20opt-out%20request&body=GitHub%20username%3A%20%0AWhat%20should%20change%3A%20';
let coverage = null;
let validation = null;
try {
  coverage = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(path.resolve(process.cwd(), 'public', 'data', 'coverage.json'), 'utf-8'));
} catch (e) { coverage = null; }
try {
  validation = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(path.resolve(process.cwd(), 'public', 'data', 'validation.json'), 'utf-8'));
} catch (e) { validation = null; }
const signals = coverage && coverage.signals
  ? Object.entries(coverage.signals).map(([key, value]) => ({ key, ...value }))
  : [];
const components = [
  ['Pushes', 'Every PushEvent the developer triggered. One push is one event, however many commits it carried.'],
  ['Merged pull requests', 'Pull requests merged by the developer in the window.'],
  ['Closed issues', 'Issues the developer closed.'],
  ['PR reviews', 'Pull-request reviews submitted.'],
  ['Issue comments', 'Comments on issues and pull requests.'],
  ['Releases', 'Releases published.'],
];
---
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Methodology &amp; API — RealGitRank</title>
    <meta name="description" content="How RealGitRank ranks GitHub developers: one absolute number — public pushes, merged pull requests, closed issues, PR reviews, issue comments and releases over 12 months. No percentiles, no weights." />
    <link rel="canonical" href={`${SITE}/methodology/`} />
    {import.meta.env.PUBLIC_GOATCOUNTER_CODE && (
      <script is:inline async src="https://gc.zgo.at/count.js" data-goatcounter={`https://${import.meta.env.PUBLIC_GOATCOUNTER_CODE}.goatcounter.com/count`}></script>
    )}
    <meta property="og:title" content="Methodology &amp; API — RealGitRank" />
    <meta property="og:description" content="One absolute activity number over 12 months: the score, the data sources, the limits and the static JSON endpoints." />
    <meta property="og:type" content="article" />
    <meta property="og:url" content={`${SITE}/methodology/`} />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'TechArticle',
      headline: 'RealGitRank methodology and API',
      url: `${SITE}/methodology/`,
      about: 'Public GitHub activity ranking',
    })} />
    <style>
      :root { --bg: #0b0f19; --panel: #131a2b; --ink: #e8ecf4; --muted: #8a93a6; --accent: #f59e0b; --accent-2: #14b8a6; --line: #1f2740; }
      body { font-family: "IBM Plex Sans", "Segoe UI", system-ui, sans-serif; max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 28px 20px 64px; background: var(--bg); color: var(--ink); line-height: 1.6; }
      h1 { font-size: 2.3rem; margin: 0 0 4px; letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
      h1 span { color: var(--accent); }
      h2 { font-size: 1.15rem; margin: 32px 0 10px; }
      h3 { font-size: 1rem; margin: 20px 0 6px; }
      .sub { color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 24px; }
      a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none; }
      a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
      .panel { background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 20px; margin: 16px 0; }
      .formula { font-size: 1.02rem; background: var(--bg); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px; overflow-x: auto; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
      .formula strong { color: var(--accent-2); }
      table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14px; margin: 10px 0 4px; }
      th, td { text-align: left; padding: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); vertical-align: top; }
      th { color: var(--muted); font-size: 11px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; }
      code, pre { font-family: ui-monospace, "Cascadia Code", Consolas, monospace; font-size: 12.5px; }
      code { background: var(--bg); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 4px; padding: 1px 5px; }
      pre { background: var(--bg); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 14px; overflow-x: auto; }
      ul { padding-left: 20px; }
      li { margin: 5px 0; }
      .note { color: var(--muted); font-size: 13px; }
      .endpoint { font-family: ui-monospace, Consolas, monospace; font-size: 13px; color: var(--accent-2); }
      :focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 2px; }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <a href="/">← Leaderboard</a>
    <h1>Methodology &amp; <span>API</span></h1>
    <p class="sub">One number decides the rank, and it is the same number for everyone.</p>

    <h2>The score</h2>
    <div class="formula">
      Activity = <strong>pushes + merged pull requests + closed issues + PR reviews + issue comments + releases</strong><br />
      over the rolling 12-month window
    </div>
    <p>The score is an absolute count of public GitHub activity events. It is not percentile-ranked, not weighted and not scaled. Every public row publishes all six components, so anyone can add them up and reproduce the score exactly. Rank is that number sorted descending; ties are broken by merged pull requests, then by login.</p>
    <p>Because the score is absolute, it does not move when the population grows — it only changes when the developer's own public activity changes. An account with no observed public activity in the window is not published at all; it is never shown as a zero and never carries a made-up rank.</p>

    <h2>The six components</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>What it counts</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {components.map(([name, what]) => (
          <tr><td><strong>{name}</strong></td><td>{what}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">Bots are skipped at ingest: logins ending in <code>[bot]</code>, <code>github-actions</code>, <code>dependabot</code>, <code>renovate[bot]</code> and GitHub's own <code>Copilot</code> agent account never enter the counts. CI pushes into a user-owned repository are not credited to the owner either — that is the bot's work, not the account's.</p>

    <h2>What GitHub counts differently</h2>
    <p>GitHub's own contribution count on a profile counts <em>commits</em>, plus pull requests, issues and reviews, and it includes private work the public feed never sees. It is not reproducible from public events, so RealGitRank does not pretend to reproduce it. The site shows it separately, labelled <code>contributions_1y</code>, for the top 10,000 developers and on profile lookup, fetched from the GitHub GraphQL API. It is context, never part of the score.</p>
    <p class="note">GitHub also trimmed public <code>PushEvent</code> payloads: a push now carries only its ref and commit hashes, not the commit list. That is why the score counts pushes rather than commits, and why no commit number is estimated.</p>

    <h2>What is actually measured</h2>
    {coverage ? (
      <>
        <p class="note">Computed from the published snapshot: {(coverage.users_ranked || coverage.users_total || coverage.users).toLocaleString()} ranked developers, window {coverage.window_start} → {coverage.window_end} ({coverage.window_days} days across {coverage.window_months ?? 0} monthly shards), score version <code>{coverage.score_version}</code>. The table below states how many of the published rows have each component above zero.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Rows with a non-zero value</th><th>Share</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {signals.map((signal) => (
              <tr><td>{signal.label}</td><td>{signal.observed.toLocaleString()}</td><td>{signal.pct}%</td></tr>
            ))}
            <tr><td>Country known</td><td>{coverage.country.known.toLocaleString()}</td><td>{coverage.country.pct}%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Primary language known</td><td>{coverage.language.known.toLocaleString()}</td><td>{coverage.language.pct}%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="note">Country and language come from optional GitHub API enrichment, which is rate-limited; the country/language filters only cover that subset.</p>
        {coverage.source ? (
          <>
            <h3>Source coverage</h3>
            <p class="note">Daily events come from <a href="https://www.gharchive.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">gharchive.org</a>. It is a partial mirror of public GitHub events, so a developer whose activity the mirror did not carry cannot be ranked — that is a source limitation, not a judgement. These numbers state how much of the stream the newest daily shards actually saw.</p>
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Days measured</td><td>{coverage.source.days_measured} of {coverage.source.days_in_window}</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Hourly files ingested</td><td>{coverage.source.hourly_files.toLocaleString()}</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Public events seen</td><td>{coverage.source.events.toLocaleString()}</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Bot events skipped</td><td>{coverage.source.bot_events.toLocaleString()} ({coverage.source.bot_share_pct}%)</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </>
        ) : null}
      </>
    ) : (
      <p class="note">Coverage data is generated by the pipeline; run it to populate this table.</p>
    )}

    <h2>Window, storage and movement</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Each run ingests one day of GH Archive events and adds it to <code>data/activity/monthly/&lt;YYYY-MM&gt;.json.gz</code>. Days already recorded are skipped, so a re-run never double counts.</li>
      <li>The score reads the current month plus the newest 12 <em>complete</em> monthly files (13 shards), so the window is never a month short. Anything older is pruned.</li>
      <li>Months older than the daily feed's retention can be filled from BigQuery (<code>githubarchive.month.*</code>) with the same event definitions; the backfill is idempotent and only writes complete past months.</li>
      <li>Each run stores the top 1,000 in <code>data/history/&lt;YYYY-MM-DD&gt;.json</code>. <code>rank_delta</code> / <code>score_delta</code> compare with the previous snapshot and are omitted for new or returning developers.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Independent validation</h2>
    {validation ? (
      <div class="panel">
        <p><strong>{validation.match_rate}% of counted merge events confirmed</strong> by GitHub ({validation.checked_at.slice(0, 10)}): {validation.events_confirmed} of {validation.events_checked} checked events across {validation.users_with_events} developers, {validation.events_unknown} unknown (private/deleted).</p>
        <p class="note">Weekly spot check of the top 50 plus a deterministic random sample; the full event list is in <a href="/data/validation.json">/data/validation.json</a>.</p>
      </div>
    ) : (
      <p class="note">The weekly validation job has not produced a report yet. Until then, treat merge counts as unvalidated.</p>
    )}

    <h2>Static JSON API</h2>
    <p>All endpoints are pre-built files on GitHub Pages, so they are cacheable and cost nothing to serve.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a class="endpoint" href="/api/leaderboard.json">/api/leaderboard.json</a> — every ranked developer in one document: <code>generated_at</code>, <code>score_unit</code>, <code>activity_components</code>, <code>window</code>, <code>url_templates</code> and a <code>users</code> array.</li>
      <li><a class="endpoint" href="/api/user/sirine2907.json">/api/user/&lt;username&gt;.json</a> — one developer's record for the top 1,000, including <code>history</code>, <code>company</code>, <code>location</code>, <code>bio</code> and the GitHub-reported <code>contributions_1y</code>. Outside the top 1,000 there is no per-user file; read that row from the chunks.</li>
      <li><a class="endpoint" href="/data/chunks/index.json">/data/chunks/index.json</a> — pagination metadata for <code>/data/chunks/page_N.json</code> (250 rows per page).</li>
      <li><a class="endpoint" href="/data/search/index.json">/data/search/index.json</a> — search manifest: <code>total</code>, <code>detailed</code>, the bucket keys and the name index.</li>
      <li><a class="endpoint" href="/data/search/e.json">/data/search/&lt;first-char&gt;.json</a> — <code>[username, rank, score, state, page]</code>; <code>score</code> is the 12-month activity count and <code>state</code> is always <code>ranked</code>.</li>
      <li><a class="endpoint" href="/data/search/names.json">/data/search/names.json</a> — rank-ordered <code>[username, rank]</code> for the top 50,000, so search can match inside a name.</li>
      <li><a class="endpoint" href="/data/coverage.json">/data/coverage.json</a> — observed counts for every component, source coverage and the validation summary.</li>
      <li><a class="endpoint" href="/badges/default.svg">/badges/&lt;username&gt;.svg</a> — README badge; every ranked developer is rendered by the badge service at <a class="endpoint" href="https://realgitrank-badges-ganx3c.freebuff.site/">realgitrank-badges-ganx3c.freebuff.site/badges/&lt;username&gt;.svg</a>.</li>
      <li><a class="endpoint" href="/llms.txt">/llms.txt</a> — a short plain-text guide for LLM and agent consumers.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Example record</h3>
<pre>&#123;
  "username": "sirine2907",
  "rank": 1,
  "rank_score": 412,
  "activity": 412,
  "score_version": "v3-activity",
  "total_pushes": 240,
  "merged_prs": 86,
  "closed_issues": 41,
  "pr_reviews": 30,
  "issue_comments": 12,
  "releases": 3,
  "country": "United States",
  "primary_language": "Dart",
  "contributions_1y": 5120,
  "profile_url": "{SITE}/profile/sirine2907",
  "badge_url": "{SITE}/badges/sirine2907.svg",
  "api_url": "{SITE}/api/user/sirine2907.json"
&#125;</pre>
    <p class="note">Field names follow the pipeline schema; optional fields are omitted rather than set to null. Please link back to {SITE.replace('https://', '')} when you publish a rank or score.</p>

    <h2 id="corrections">Corrections, removal and disclaimer</h2>
    <p>RealGitRank is an independent, unofficial project built only from public GitHub events. A score is an automated count over one rolling 12-month window — not a statement of fact about a person, not a measure of skill or worth, and not affiliated with GitHub or an endorsement of anyone.</p>
    <p>If an entry is wrong, misattributed, or you would rather not appear, open a request with the username and what should change: <a href={CORRECTION_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">correction or removal request</a>. Removals are applied on the next daily publish, after which the entry disappears from the leaderboard, the JSON API, the public chunks and the badge endpoint.</p>

    <h2>Related</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/">Global leaderboard</a></li>
      <li><a href="/badges/">README badges</a></li>
      <li><a href="/countries">Country leaderboards</a></li>
      <li><a href={CORRECTION_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Correction or removal request</a></li>
    </ul>
  </body>
</html>
